<!--l version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!—
    Debug only. This file lives in src/debug, so it is not part of a release build and the
    release APK keeps Android's default: cleartext HTTP refused outright.

    WHY IT IS NEEDED

    Since API 28 cleartext is blocked unless an app opts in, and this app targets 36. The debug
    build talks to http://10.0.2.2:3000 — the loopback alias the emulator maps to the developer's
    own machine — so without this every request fails before a socket is opened. It fails
    silently, too: OkHttp raises it as an IOException like any other, so the app cannot tell it
    apart from a server being down.

    That is exactly how it presented on 10 September 2026. Tapping "Send me the code" did nothing
    at all, the flow never advanced, and there was nothing in logcat, because the request was
    refused by policy before it left the process.

    Scoped to the two hosts a developer machine actually answers on, rather than
    cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" across the board. A blanket permission in a debug build is
    how a debug build ends up trusting a coffee-shop network.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The emulator's alias for the host machine's loopback. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
        <!-- A device on the same network, or a physical device over adb reverse. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
